00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:37,100
And with that, if everybody's ready, we'll start off first public hearing for tonight, which is a continued public hearing for an application for a subdivision of land submitted by NTV Builders, LLC.

89
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:43,720
This property is located at Tax Map 2, Lot 26, also known as 112 Little Mill Road.

90
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:54,540
This application proposes to develop the subject property to construct 21 single-family dwelling units into a residential subdivision called NOAA Estates.

91
00:08:54,540 --> 00:08:56,640
Hi, Mr. Lavelle.

92
00:08:57,060 --> 00:08:58,860
Good evening, members of the board.

93
00:08:58,940 --> 00:09:07,840
For the record, Ryan Lavelle with Lavelle Associates, representing NTV Builders, LLC, for the proposed NOAA Estates development at 112 Little Mill Road.

94
00:09:07,840 --> 00:09:14,280
This project has come a long way and has now been before the board several times now.

95
00:09:14,980 --> 00:09:21,660
The property itself has also appeared before the town in various forms over the years, but it seems like we really got a winner here.

96
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:31,700
This proposal is a 21-unit open space subdivision using a zero-lot line condominium form of ownership on approximately 62.75 acres.

97
00:09:32,760 --> 00:09:36,100
Each single-family dwelling will be served by an individual well and septic.

98
00:09:36,200 --> 00:09:45,020
The development will be accessed by two proposed private streets being shown, I believe, as Tanner and Vince.

99
00:09:45,020 --> 00:09:59,840
Since the last meeting, the plan's drainage report, yield information, and supporting materials have been substantially revised, hence why I handed out copies of the plan when I walked in.

100
00:10:01,460 --> 00:10:08,820
Engineering Alliance has worked through town comments, AOT comments, and updated much of the material.

101
00:10:08,820 --> 00:10:15,380
We received the second round of K&A review, I believe, today.

102
00:10:16,140 --> 00:10:24,100
Started going through that and didn't see any big red flags as far as being able to fulfill those comments.

103
00:10:25,980 --> 00:10:29,620
I'm not going to talk about the conditional use permit because that would be the next one.

104
00:10:29,620 --> 00:10:39,880
At this point, we are primarily wanting to update the board on the considerable progress that has been made and clearly identify the remaining items,

105
00:10:40,580 --> 00:10:47,860
which would be final sign-off for engineering review, state subdivision approval, alteration of terrain,

106
00:10:47,860 --> 00:10:55,740
and the conservation commission process to move forward so that the cup can be issued.

107
00:10:58,700 --> 00:11:04,640
The other thing that I did want to note that I saw on the K&A letter was town council review.

108
00:11:05,780 --> 00:11:10,880
I had sent the condo docs to the town a month or two ago.

109
00:11:10,880 --> 00:11:18,520
I know that there's been turnover at the planner position, so I don't know if it ever got sent to Diane for review.

110
00:11:18,680 --> 00:11:22,400
Maybe Steve might have a better idea if that's the case.

111
00:11:22,580 --> 00:11:24,060
I have no idea.

112
00:11:24,860 --> 00:11:32,220
I think it might be good for me to re-forward those documents and make sure that they get over to Diane because we've had them prepared for a while.

113
00:11:32,220 --> 00:11:44,740
Yeah, I mean, obviously Andrea has access to all those records as well as Nicole's e-mail, so I think a simple inquiry.

114
00:11:45,020 --> 00:11:46,940
Yeah, I can reach out to her about that.

115
00:11:47,120 --> 00:11:55,520
It would be appropriate, and if it hasn't occurred, which Nicole was pretty good about getting stuff out,

116
00:11:55,740 --> 00:11:58,820
and Diane is equally good about getting stuff back.

117
00:11:58,820 --> 00:12:03,800
So if it's been that length, I think that there's some sort of lapse in communication there.

118
00:12:03,960 --> 00:12:05,000
Well, I'm not sure there is.

119
00:12:05,060 --> 00:12:07,300
It could be sitting in a response from Diane.

120
00:12:07,520 --> 00:12:10,800
Could it just as easily be sitting in an inbox downstairs?

121
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:27,560
We had another matter on last month's agenda on the KRKR matter that the board sought input relative to potential issue with notification of the variance.

122
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:29,940
Yes, there was a scrivener's error made, right?

123
00:12:30,140 --> 00:12:30,340
Mm-hmm.

124
00:12:30,860 --> 00:12:43,300
And Nicole sent that out at our request to Diane, like within a day or two, and got an answer almost immediately.

125
00:12:43,700 --> 00:12:46,580
So, I mean, that's just Diane's style.

126
00:12:46,580 --> 00:12:50,200
And one last thing about the K&A letter.

127
00:12:50,700 --> 00:12:55,920
I think on the first page it states all the state approvals that are necessary on it,

128
00:12:56,220 --> 00:12:59,700
and I just wanted a little bit of clarification because it still says shoreland permit,

129
00:12:59,840 --> 00:13:02,760
but no construction is happening within 250 feet.

130
00:13:02,840 --> 00:13:05,160
We don't need a shoreland permit, correct?

131
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:07,040
If you're not doing any work.

132
00:13:07,420 --> 00:13:07,620
Yeah.

133
00:13:07,620 --> 00:13:09,620
I think we're 500, 600 feet away.

134
00:13:09,820 --> 00:13:22,700
Then what I would ask you to do on the drawings is put a line that defines what the limits of the shoreland protection district is.

135
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:25,560
I'm pretty sure they're on here.

136
00:13:26,180 --> 00:13:27,300
Yeah, I thought that they were.

137
00:13:27,540 --> 00:13:31,040
No, they have the lines that shows where the reference line is.

138
00:13:31,080 --> 00:13:32,220
Oh, that's a reference line.

139
00:13:32,220 --> 00:13:40,040
You have your natural woodland buffer, you have your 150 foot, and you have your 250 foot.

140
00:13:40,200 --> 00:13:43,000
Yep, I only have the 250 foot shoreline reference line.

141
00:13:43,120 --> 00:13:43,520
I understand.

142
00:13:43,940 --> 00:13:44,220
All right.

143
00:13:44,460 --> 00:13:45,300
Yep, thank you, Steve.

144
00:13:45,480 --> 00:13:46,120
I appreciate it.

145
00:13:46,140 --> 00:13:47,520
So, what's the difference between the two?

146
00:13:48,580 --> 00:13:58,480
Well, under the Comprehensive Shoreland Protection Act of the state, right, certain streams and bodies of water, you know,

147
00:13:59,060 --> 00:14:01,260
the Shull Pond is on the list, right?

148
00:14:01,260 --> 00:14:03,960
It's afforded protection by that.

149
00:14:04,460 --> 00:14:12,580
A portion of this property that is scheduled to be open space is situated in the protected district.

150
00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:23,400
And any work within 250 feet of the reference line requires a permit.

151
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:40,660
And in this particular case, if you recall, there was an earlier version of a development plan for this same piece of land that was ultimately withdrawn that contemplated work within 250.

152
00:14:40,660 --> 00:14:41,660
Yes.

153
00:14:41,660 --> 00:14:50,660
And so, everything was set up on this when the plan came back to suggest that that permit would be necessary.

154
00:14:51,280 --> 00:14:59,860
I agree with Ryan that it's not, but I want him to say it and demonstrate it so that we don't connect the dots for somebody.

155
00:15:00,040 --> 00:15:01,000
I completely understand.

156
00:15:01,280 --> 00:15:02,020
I appreciate it.

157
00:15:02,280 --> 00:15:02,680
Thank you.

158
00:15:02,820 --> 00:15:03,660
I didn't.

159
00:15:03,880 --> 00:15:04,120
All right.

160
00:15:04,120 --> 00:15:06,560
So, can you consider those dots connected?

161
00:15:07,120 --> 00:15:07,300
Yes.

162
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:07,880
Thank you.

163
00:15:09,820 --> 00:15:14,420
All right.

164
00:15:16,360 --> 00:15:16,800
Okay.

165
00:15:16,920 --> 00:15:18,340
So, I just want to double check something.

166
00:15:19,100 --> 00:15:19,540
Yes.

167
00:15:19,780 --> 00:15:21,780
To make sure I'm not sitting on anything I shouldn't.

168
00:15:21,780 --> 00:15:24,840
Was this over at zoning, this new version?

169
00:15:24,960 --> 00:15:25,420
It wasn't.

170
00:15:25,660 --> 00:15:25,780
Nothing.

171
00:15:25,980 --> 00:15:26,160
No.

172
00:15:26,300 --> 00:15:27,120
It's never been at zoning.

173
00:15:27,240 --> 00:15:30,160
Only here and conservation for the cup.

174
00:15:30,660 --> 00:15:30,980
Okay.

175
00:15:31,160 --> 00:15:32,900
I just want to make sure I don't have to recuse.

176
00:15:33,020 --> 00:15:33,180
No.

177
00:15:33,680 --> 00:15:34,020
You don't.

178
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:34,540
Beautiful.

179
00:15:35,480 --> 00:15:35,860
All right.

180
00:15:36,200 --> 00:15:38,460
So, is that everything you wanted to say up to this point?

181
00:15:38,460 --> 00:15:39,060
Yeah, that's everything.

182
00:15:39,180 --> 00:15:41,900
If you guys have any questions, I'd be happy to jot them down.

183
00:15:42,060 --> 00:15:45,880
And like I said, we're progressing really nicely on this one.

184
00:15:45,880 --> 00:15:51,080
As you can see, the K&A letter went from yay thick to, you know, less so.

185
00:15:51,500 --> 00:15:51,740
Yeah.

186
00:15:51,880 --> 00:15:52,660
I've had it before.

187
00:15:53,060 --> 00:15:56,540
All the state permits are rolling at their speed that they're rolling at.

188
00:15:56,840 --> 00:16:03,320
And we're going to be scheduling a site walk, which is the missing piece for the conservation

189
00:16:03,320 --> 00:16:04,460
to give you guys comments.

190
00:16:05,520 --> 00:16:07,900
Hopefully not next week, but the week following.

191
00:16:08,500 --> 00:16:13,160
And then they'll be able to issue you some correspondence, I hope, in time for the next planning

192
00:16:13,160 --> 00:16:17,480
board meeting so that we can maybe move even closer than tonight.

193
00:16:17,960 --> 00:16:18,220
Okay.

194
00:16:18,840 --> 00:16:19,220
All right.

195
00:16:19,260 --> 00:16:19,660
Thank you.

196
00:16:20,520 --> 00:16:23,220
Steve, do you have anything you want to move on?

197
00:16:23,220 --> 00:16:28,320
I'll give you a short overview of my letter report that you should have in your packet dated

198
00:16:28,320 --> 00:16:29,700
September 14th.

199
00:16:29,700 --> 00:16:30,140
Yep.

200
00:16:32,780 --> 00:16:38,160
We had previously commented on an earlier version of these plans back on June 16th.

201
00:16:38,160 --> 00:16:50,080
And on September 1st, we were provided with a 22 drawings updated together with a yield plan

202
00:16:50,080 --> 00:16:55,220
of five drawings and an updated drainage report, stormwater management plan.

203
00:16:56,380 --> 00:16:58,020
Quite a bit of information.

204
00:16:59,100 --> 00:16:59,780
Highlights.

205
00:16:59,900 --> 00:17:04,960
I'm not going to take you through every remark, but highlights are the state permits, general

206
00:17:04,960 --> 00:17:05,840
comment one.

207
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:11,820
Those are the ones absent the shoreland permit that they're going to be not needed.

208
00:17:13,440 --> 00:17:17,240
General comment number two, top of page two.

209
00:17:17,660 --> 00:17:23,960
There's the bonding scenario that I would look at upon any motion for approval.

210
00:17:24,220 --> 00:17:25,280
That's very standard.

211
00:17:25,280 --> 00:17:38,700
Third bullet is basically carrying forward remarks given by Chief Devine relative to fire protection.

212
00:17:39,340 --> 00:17:43,660
This project is going to have a 30,000-gallon cistern.

213
00:17:43,660 --> 00:18:04,300
And he asked that when the time came, if your board would stipulate a condition requiring that that to be in and serviceable prior to the issuance of building permits.

214
00:18:04,300 --> 00:18:08,940
And we'll deal with that at another time.

215
00:18:08,940 --> 00:18:28,300
Remark number four, if you have a copy of the plan, notice that to the east of where the actual entrance would be constructed, there's a couple of poems that belong to other folks.

216
00:18:28,300 --> 00:18:39,900
And then there is an extension of what otherwise looks like right-of-way, it's not, it was never platted as right-of-way, it just looks like that.

217
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,420
It was never a dedication, public dedication.

218
00:18:43,140 --> 00:18:44,560
It goes back out to the middle.

219
00:18:44,560 --> 00:18:54,940
That piece of land is part of the subject property and is intended to become part of the open space.

220
00:18:55,060 --> 00:19:07,520
Nothing will be built there and it won't be relied upon in any way to support construction that's part of the subject property or the condominium, as it's a zero-lot-lot condominium.

221
00:19:07,520 --> 00:19:27,720
That said, the houses that abut that 50-foot parcel of land through the east and the west, each have their driveway access to Little Mill Road within the frontage of that strip of land.

222
00:19:27,720 --> 00:19:51,060
That was intentional because when those two houses were built, they came out of the same piece of property that this is a residual of and there was contemplation of, this is well over a decade ago, of continuing the subdivision one day and building a road and it never happened.

223
00:19:51,060 --> 00:20:10,360
The reason I raised this is where this is going to become open space in the condominium, if there is not existing easement rights over that land for the benefit of those two homeowners, I would want to see them granted at this time so that we don't end up with

224
00:20:10,360 --> 00:20:25,360
an encumbrance on an encumbrance on the open space, you know, a title conflict, that could effectively and inadvertently affect three parties, you know, the two existing homeowners.

225
00:20:25,940 --> 00:20:30,420
You can just get an easement there from Mr. Story and take care of that.

226
00:20:31,180 --> 00:20:31,280
Yep.

227
00:20:31,500 --> 00:20:31,820
All right.

228
00:20:31,900 --> 00:20:39,500
Under zoning matters, really one of the things that, you know, if you look at the plan,

229
00:20:40,360 --> 00:20:59,900
you know, this is a fairly good representation of one of the choices that we intentionally put options in when we built the open space development ordinance about 15 years ago that we wanted to see.

230
00:20:59,900 --> 00:21:19,900
And part of one of the reasons that we did this at that time was there was a fledgling piece of statute out there that was just been adopted and signed into law that was originally going to take effect in 2009.

231
00:21:19,900 --> 00:21:27,040
It was delayed effective date to 2010, and that is the workforce housing statute, right?

232
00:21:28,040 --> 00:21:49,200
So by this zero lot line land condominium is really what this is, compresses the available residential density to a small portion of the premises.

233
00:21:49,900 --> 00:22:04,880
In this particular case, that has secondary benefit because of proximity of the land on the southerly portion of the property, which is the majority of it, to Shoal Pond.

234
00:22:05,240 --> 00:22:13,780
I mean, effectively, this creates conservation land adjacent to Shoal Pond that will be preserved in that fashion in perpetuity.

235
00:22:13,780 --> 00:22:28,120
But one of the requirements for the open space development ordinance is a 50-foot perimeter buffer.

236
00:22:28,120 --> 00:22:41,100
Now, remember, out front, there's the lots that came out of the parent parcel, you know, decades past that have existing homes on them.

237
00:22:41,260 --> 00:22:49,520
Around the periphery of those where they share a common boundary with the development track, there's a 50-foot natural buffer.

238
00:22:49,520 --> 00:23:00,420
And under the language of the ordinance, that perimeter buffer shall be comprised of existing and planted vegetation.

239
00:23:00,420 --> 00:23:07,260
And as my remark continues, the italics is taken directly from the ordinance.

240
00:23:07,260 --> 00:23:26,420
In the case of an open space development with a zero lot line layout arrangement, no buildings, pavement, above-ground utility installations, or other permanent improvements shall be situated within any portion of the required perimeter landscape buffer unless so approved by the planning board.

241
00:23:26,420 --> 00:23:33,600
So, it's basically the setup to make sure that we had a viable buffer.

242
00:23:33,600 --> 00:23:43,680
However, as a safety net, our board can use your, you know, authority under innovative zoning.

243
00:23:43,980 --> 00:23:52,920
This, that, the open space development ordinance was adopted under authority of RSA 67421 as an innovative zoning technique.

244
00:23:52,920 --> 00:24:07,060
So, rather than running off to the ZBA to get some variance for some aspect of it that's tied to the OSD ordinance, your board has the authority to grant dispensation if you feel appropriate.

245
00:24:07,980 --> 00:24:16,000
In this particular case, if you take a look at the plans in a careful way, you'll notice that there's graded embankment.

246
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:31,300
Some of the building sites, again, this is effectively a land condominium, adjoined the natural buffer.

247
00:24:31,300 --> 00:24:37,940
And there's grading that goes into the buffer, and that's pretty easy to replant.

248
00:24:37,940 --> 00:24:49,540
And there's also half a dozen private wells that are contemplated being within, installed within that buffer.

249
00:24:49,540 --> 00:24:56,840
So, at some point, you know, probably not tonight, but, you know, I'm going to suggest that you take a pretty good look at the plans

250
00:24:56,840 --> 00:25:05,680
and have Ryan give you a detailed explanation of the extent of activity that they would be providing or proposing within the buffer

251
00:25:05,680 --> 00:25:10,400
and what mitigation for that would be pro-offered, right?

252
00:25:10,400 --> 00:25:16,460
Obviously, grading of embankment, not a big deal.

253
00:25:18,260 --> 00:25:24,000
Bring it to a line and grade that is probably superior to what's there today and do a planting plan.

254
00:25:24,100 --> 00:25:29,960
The question is, from the board's perspective, is how robust does that want to be, in my opinion?

255
00:25:29,960 --> 00:25:37,800
And then, obviously, there is going to have to be incursion into that buffer, not only in the initial instance,

256
00:25:37,920 --> 00:25:42,820
to install any drilled wells, but also potentially to maintain the wellheads at some point.

257
00:25:43,700 --> 00:25:53,400
So, those are things that would need to be thought about before that dispensation that they're going to need can be given.

258
00:25:55,500 --> 00:25:58,420
Continuing at the top of page three with zoning matters,

259
00:25:58,420 --> 00:26:01,880
I'm going to skip down to the third item.

260
00:26:04,520 --> 00:26:09,800
Again, it comes back to the zero lot line layout and condominium form of ownership.

261
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:19,940
As Ryan indicated, draft copies of those documents have been submitted, presumably, and we need to chase those down.

262
00:26:20,900 --> 00:26:24,800
That always seems like a formality to people, but in the case of condominiums,

263
00:26:24,800 --> 00:26:28,900
with maintenance of open space, it's not.

264
00:26:29,020 --> 00:26:29,940
It's not at all.

265
00:26:30,060 --> 00:26:35,700
There's a lot of provisions in there that, you know, like we saw at the villas, right, recently,

266
00:26:35,940 --> 00:26:42,980
where there were some special provisions in there that ended up with the town of Sandown

267
00:26:42,980 --> 00:26:48,920
getting fee simple title, the seven acres of land that was very important to the Conservation Commission, right?

268
00:26:48,920 --> 00:26:56,100
So, on this, as far as the protection of this open space and management of this open space,

269
00:26:56,180 --> 00:27:02,740
I suspect that will be one of the conversations Ryan may have with the Conservation Commission upcoming.

270
00:27:02,920 --> 00:27:08,940
So, we want to keep that a little fluid until everybody that has opportunity to weigh in we've heard from, right?

271
00:27:08,940 --> 00:27:17,120
So, the fourth item is more of a technical item.

272
00:27:17,640 --> 00:27:19,620
We need a few more test bits, that type of thing.

273
00:27:21,020 --> 00:27:27,760
And the fifth item on zoning matters is the conditional use permit.

274
00:27:27,760 --> 00:27:37,900
Obviously, there is a proposed proposal to impact 1,645 square feet.

275
00:27:40,160 --> 00:27:45,200
And Ryan will be taking that up, I understand, with the Conservation Commission in a couple of weeks,

276
00:27:45,320 --> 00:27:47,360
and they'll weigh in and get back to us with that.

277
00:27:47,360 --> 00:27:57,060
And so, until we have that happy letter from the Conservation Commission,

278
00:27:57,060 --> 00:28:01,620
that's going to stay on the sideline, I guess, would be your custom, right?

279
00:28:01,920 --> 00:28:02,180
Okay.

280
00:28:03,220 --> 00:28:07,660
Under planning and design matters, I'm not going to talk about many of these,

281
00:28:07,760 --> 00:28:13,080
because a lot of these are just technical items that Ryan and his consulting engineer,

282
00:28:13,180 --> 00:28:14,520
I'm certain, will take care of.

283
00:28:14,520 --> 00:28:23,960
But on page four, item four, there is a waiver request here under section 9.23,

284
00:28:24,820 --> 00:28:30,780
customary request for waiver of on-site recreational opportunities.

285
00:28:31,540 --> 00:28:36,700
And Ryan can speak to that, because he has a proposal for an alternative means of providing that,

286
00:28:36,780 --> 00:28:39,340
that he'll present at the appropriate time.

287
00:28:39,340 --> 00:28:48,340
The fifth remark, when the application first came in, similar to the prior application,

288
00:28:49,100 --> 00:28:55,140
it presumed that the total length of Dead End Street was going to exceed 1,000 feet.

289
00:28:57,440 --> 00:29:04,340
In my memo of June 16th, I questioned that, because I didn't think it was.

290
00:29:04,340 --> 00:29:11,480
And they came back and demonstrated that it's 976.61 feet.

291
00:29:11,580 --> 00:29:13,560
So that waiver request can be withdrawn.

292
00:29:14,100 --> 00:29:14,240
Okay.

293
00:29:14,800 --> 00:29:21,640
As a practical matter, after, I forget what the effective date is, in 2027,

294
00:29:22,640 --> 00:29:29,660
you're not going to, municipalities will no longer be able to have maximum dead-end lengths.

295
00:29:29,660 --> 00:29:35,900
It will all come down to the, as part of their planning and zoning ordinances,

296
00:29:36,200 --> 00:29:51,000
they will default to NFPA and be controlled locally by the authority having just jurisdiction to administer NFPA 1.

297
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:56,120
So, that's just kind of some news.

298
00:29:59,700 --> 00:30:00,860
All right.

