01:40:26,600 --> 01:40:31,900
to come here before the board, submit everything, AOT, all these things, and then go,

1391
01:40:31,900 --> 01:40:36,280
did you not see that there's 90 feet right here and our regulation says 200?

1392
01:40:36,540 --> 01:40:40,020
That goes straight to Brian's point on the earlier application, doesn't it?

1393
01:40:40,480 --> 01:40:41,480
Yes, it does.

1394
01:40:42,660 --> 01:40:48,980
It does much to a more obvious extent because, you know, grading and stuff,

1395
01:40:49,040 --> 01:40:52,980
we don't know what the grades of the road are going to be, but it's…

1396
01:40:52,980 --> 01:40:55,720
Well, I mean, what do we know, right?

1397
01:40:56,000 --> 01:40:56,280
Right.

1398
01:40:58,780 --> 01:41:01,280
You know, Armand's way that's shown on here.

1399
01:41:01,900 --> 01:41:09,280
We will conform with our road design requirements.

1400
01:41:10,520 --> 01:41:12,780
We'll have sight distance, I would think.

1401
01:41:12,880 --> 01:41:13,540
It does, yeah.

1402
01:41:15,740 --> 01:41:20,560
It would be a formalized entrance for not only the handful of lots.

1403
01:41:20,660 --> 01:41:21,400
What's this showing?

1404
01:41:21,720 --> 01:41:23,220
Five, maybe seven lots?

1405
01:41:23,240 --> 01:41:23,940
It's showing seven.

1406
01:41:24,000 --> 01:41:25,440
I'm not sure if it's even going to be that.

1407
01:41:25,760 --> 01:41:27,460
Yeah, I mean, whatever.

1408
01:41:27,460 --> 01:41:37,020
Small number of additional lots, but we would have appropriate infrastructure.

1409
01:41:37,680 --> 01:41:38,220
That's correct.

1410
01:41:38,220 --> 01:41:47,600
And then if you folks that are familiar with the area, if you look on what is conceptual lot seven,

1411
01:41:47,600 --> 01:41:56,440
there would be a connection from the new road to the existing private way,

1412
01:41:57,160 --> 01:41:58,140
the Shoal Pond Road.

1413
01:41:58,220 --> 01:41:58,480
Yes.

1414
01:41:58,480 --> 01:42:04,100
That goes through the balance of the Titorenko property to Eureka Way and beyond.

1415
01:42:04,100 --> 01:42:10,120
And I'm thinking there's probably a dozen and a half homes out there.

1416
01:42:10,140 --> 01:42:10,800
At least.

1417
01:42:11,120 --> 01:42:11,440
At least.

1418
01:42:13,920 --> 01:42:21,960
So that would remain unchanged other than there would be formalization of right away where everything is prescriptive out there today.

1419
01:42:22,540 --> 01:42:22,840
Yes.

1420
01:42:22,840 --> 01:42:31,240
And Ryan's right, you know, from research that we've done, and I've had conversations with both him and Attorney Campbell,

1421
01:42:31,240 --> 01:42:53,200
the segment of Shoal Pond that runs along and parallel with the westerly line of where Armand's Way is going out towards Little Mill would really become superfluous to anybody.

1422
01:42:53,840 --> 01:42:58,520
You know, you can't close it, but you can make it obsolete.

1423
01:42:58,520 --> 01:43:01,420
Yeah, it'll just go away on its own.

1424
01:43:01,500 --> 01:43:05,080
It'll just become those driveways because everybody's going to use that main.

1425
01:43:05,440 --> 01:43:08,540
Obviously, you know what team I'm on and why I'm here.

1426
01:43:08,940 --> 01:43:14,540
But, you know, if I was living down there and I could choose to go on this paved section to get out, especially in the winter,

1427
01:43:14,880 --> 01:43:20,340
or go up and down the, you'd have to drive down here to see what the gravel section looks like.

1428
01:43:20,560 --> 01:43:22,940
But it's not even a country road.

1429
01:43:22,980 --> 01:43:24,420
It's not a discussion, really.

1430
01:43:24,420 --> 01:43:33,000
As your town engineer, I look at this as not so much a land use matter, but as a town engineering issue, DPW, public works issue,

1431
01:43:33,140 --> 01:43:39,820
this solution, if this was built, would be much better from a public safety operation standpoint.

1432
01:43:41,080 --> 01:43:42,220
It would be much better.

1433
01:43:42,220 --> 01:43:53,640
And obviously why we got here, and I alluded to it before, but generational cottages and stuff, I'm not going to, I have, I had the family stuff in my head a lot when I was doing the planning board thing.

1434
01:43:53,640 --> 01:43:59,160
But in essence, the clients that I'm working for, it says prepared for, Annette Teterinco.

1435
01:43:59,340 --> 01:44:01,780
The other one is Cecile, two sisters that own it together.

1436
01:44:02,060 --> 01:44:02,980
And there's a brother too.

1437
01:44:02,980 --> 01:44:13,500
But their father built one cottage in there, and then built another one, and then said, these family can live here.

1438
01:44:13,860 --> 01:44:17,820
And I think they were traveling, I forget if it was, travelist, circus people.

1439
01:44:18,640 --> 01:44:25,820
And eventually it just got to the point where there's tons of these cottages, and then they started conveying outside of the family.

1440
01:44:25,820 --> 01:44:32,880
And then it eventually got caught up with, you know, putting postage stamp lots around each one of these cottages.

1441
01:44:33,140 --> 01:44:37,360
And then there were six owned by the family directly that never got those postage stamp.

1442
01:44:37,420 --> 01:44:40,460
And that's why we came to the planning board and got the lot separated.

1443
01:44:40,760 --> 01:44:44,880
So we're, we're trying to clean up the carnival, no pun intended.

1444
01:44:45,600 --> 01:44:55,800
And this is another way to clean it up out here for both safety and, you know, from, from my perspective too.

1445
01:44:55,820 --> 01:45:05,340
A piece that they created that's 15 acres, that is upland of everyone, and serves no purpose.

1446
01:45:05,940 --> 01:45:08,640
And its frontage doesn't even allow for a two lot subdivision.

1447
01:45:09,280 --> 01:45:12,660
It has a frontage variance that was granted by the zoning board.

1448
01:45:13,820 --> 01:45:16,040
I think, I don't remember, and it's not listed on here.

1449
01:45:16,080 --> 01:45:18,460
It was like 142 feet of frontage or something like that.

1450
01:45:19,940 --> 01:45:22,720
So just enough for one house on 15 acres.

1451
01:45:22,720 --> 01:45:25,020
It just doesn't make, make sense.

1452
01:45:25,820 --> 01:45:31,060
So this is, you said they did some research and they can't eliminate that old road.

1453
01:45:31,120 --> 01:45:36,580
They can't just incorporate and allow the hope is that you incorporate this change.

1454
01:45:37,440 --> 01:45:41,560
And even with signs or whatever, at first people might be confused.

1455
01:45:41,560 --> 01:45:43,740
And I hope that a lot of them attend the public hearing.

1456
01:45:43,900 --> 01:45:46,480
Not many did when we were doing the other thing, if you recall.

1457
01:45:48,000 --> 01:45:50,140
But I think it will be pretty obvious what's going on.

1458
01:45:50,140 --> 01:46:02,500
They're never going to block them because, as Attorney Campbell stated, the rights are very definitive that they have the right to pass and repass in perpetuity forever on Shoal Pond Road.

1459
01:46:03,000 --> 01:46:07,100
And Shoal Pond Road can't be moved from where it physically is.

1460
01:46:07,100 --> 01:46:11,420
But like I said, we can try to encourage to reroute.

1461
01:46:12,740 --> 01:46:15,100
Hopefully that means Amazon drivers.

1462
01:46:15,880 --> 01:46:18,100
That means mail, you know.

1463
01:46:19,380 --> 01:46:20,580
Fire and safety.

1464
01:46:21,060 --> 01:46:22,520
I'm probably preaching to the choir.

1465
01:46:22,520 --> 01:46:32,580
But I think that it's a much safer thing as well out here and a nice fix for something that's going on.

1466
01:46:33,020 --> 01:46:33,600
Mr. Jim?

1467
01:46:33,900 --> 01:46:34,060
Yeah.

1468
01:46:35,340 --> 01:46:37,500
I've been down there hundreds of times.

1469
01:46:38,600 --> 01:46:40,620
Some of you probably have never been down there.

1470
01:46:41,900 --> 01:46:43,680
It's a very tight configuration.

1471
01:46:44,740 --> 01:46:45,780
Marriott of roads.

1472
01:46:46,380 --> 01:46:47,180
Not roads.

1473
01:46:47,260 --> 01:46:47,600
I'm sorry.

1474
01:46:47,700 --> 01:46:48,260
They're not roads.

1475
01:46:49,280 --> 01:46:49,960
Private ways.

1476
01:46:50,300 --> 01:46:51,960
Driveways or whatever you want to call it.

1477
01:46:51,960 --> 01:46:56,800
Any improvement like this is a blessing.

1478
01:46:57,880 --> 01:47:02,800
Not only for the fire department, police department, rescue, anybody.

1479
01:47:03,820 --> 01:47:08,120
So if you wanted to get a feel for what's down there, take a ride.

1480
01:47:09,280 --> 01:47:12,620
I encourage the board to take a ride and see this.

1481
01:47:12,620 --> 01:47:14,600
Obviously, you can't vote and make a decision anyway.

1482
01:47:14,960 --> 01:47:20,360
But if you guys check it out and you guys haven't, if you weren't on the planning board when this got subdivision approval,

1483
01:47:20,360 --> 01:47:24,560
and if you weren't on the zoning board when it got the variances that it needed,

1484
01:47:24,660 --> 01:47:30,420
I definitely encourage you to drive down that portion and maybe you can see what we're talking about.

1485
01:47:30,420 --> 01:47:42,120
There's regulations for a reason, and then there's waivers for a reason, and I think that there's a good case for some sort of something.

1486
01:47:42,120 --> 01:47:53,680
In this case, Ryan, I would support the waiver because you're effectively making Show Pond Roads access to Little Mill redundant.

1487
01:47:54,560 --> 01:47:55,500
I get that.

1488
01:47:55,500 --> 01:48:09,840
And as Eddie just said, continued use of that from a public safety standpoint in a lot of reasons is not as desirable as having new construction.

1489
01:48:10,520 --> 01:48:14,300
And it is not town-maintained anything down there.

1490
01:48:14,400 --> 01:48:18,460
It's all much like Hersey Road that was just approved moments ago by committee.

1491
01:48:18,460 --> 01:48:24,580
You may remember, it wasn't terribly a number of years ago, that we improved Little Mill Road, right?

1492
01:48:24,880 --> 01:48:27,000
We did a reclaimed, stabilized, based construction.

1493
01:48:27,280 --> 01:48:27,380
Right.

1494
01:48:27,840 --> 01:48:38,780
There were a couple of intersections of Class 5 roads with Little Mill that we did betterments on to improve the alignment and function, grading.

1495
01:48:39,620 --> 01:48:43,360
This is one we couldn't touch if we wanted to because it's a private way.

1496
01:48:43,360 --> 01:48:52,680
So, it is the same way today that it was 10 years ago before we rebuilt Little Mill Road.

1497
01:48:53,580 --> 01:48:56,980
So, that's some of my familiarity with this.

1498
01:48:57,340 --> 01:48:58,120
Brian, do you have a comment?

1499
01:48:58,280 --> 01:48:58,520
Yeah.

1500
01:48:58,880 --> 01:49:00,120
I agree with what everybody's saying.

1501
01:49:00,180 --> 01:49:00,820
I just had a quick quote.

1502
01:49:01,360 --> 01:49:07,240
So, I understand that Show Pond Road is private, and it's maintained by the current houses that they put money into a pool, or however they do it.

1503
01:49:07,440 --> 01:49:08,960
However they do it, I'm not sure.

1504
01:49:09,280 --> 01:49:09,920
However they do it.

1505
01:49:10,880 --> 01:49:12,840
I'm assuming Armand's Way would also be private.

1506
01:49:13,360 --> 01:49:15,240
I'm making that assumption.

1507
01:49:15,720 --> 01:49:16,200
Same.

1508
01:49:16,340 --> 01:49:20,860
But the problem is, too, like I alluded to.

1509
01:49:21,400 --> 01:49:21,620
Sorry.

1510
01:49:22,860 --> 01:49:23,240
Oh, I'm sorry.

1511
01:49:23,480 --> 01:49:24,360
What was private?

1512
01:49:24,420 --> 01:49:24,960
I missed it.

1513
01:49:25,140 --> 01:49:27,300
So, Show Pond Road is a private road.

1514
01:49:27,440 --> 01:49:29,780
It's maintained by the houses, the collective houses that are there.

1515
01:49:29,860 --> 01:49:32,120
However they pool their money, they maintain it.

1516
01:49:32,120 --> 01:49:37,360
I'm asking, I'm assuming that Armand's Way is also going to be private.

1517
01:49:38,060 --> 01:49:38,320
Okay.

1518
01:49:38,320 --> 01:49:43,880
Assuming these houses were built, these conceptual homes were built for Armand's Way.

1519
01:49:44,300 --> 01:49:44,760
Yes.

1520
01:49:44,760 --> 01:49:49,700
That would become a private way, maintained very much like the existing camps.

1521
01:49:50,180 --> 01:49:54,040
But now you're eliminating a section of road that is maintained by these people.

1522
01:49:54,260 --> 01:50:07,880
Would these current residences then have to take the money and maintain, would the new residences for the lower portion of Armand's Way that they would be essentially utilizing, would their responsibility for maintaining the road end at Armand's Way?

1523
01:50:07,880 --> 01:50:14,660
Yeah, so from what I understand of it, the intent probably will be to make it a private way.

1524
01:50:14,760 --> 01:50:21,140
But I may not even be the one before you presenting it to the planning board if it gets sold to, you know, John over here.

1525
01:50:21,260 --> 01:50:26,660
But what I'm trying to say is we're giving everyone down there a choice.

1526
01:50:27,080 --> 01:50:27,300
Right.

1527
01:50:27,340 --> 01:50:27,520
Right.

1528
01:50:27,800 --> 01:50:37,740
Even though everyone will come up to this crossroads and I know which way I'm going to want to drive my car, they still would have the option to take the left and take the road less traveled.

1529
01:50:37,880 --> 01:50:38,340
If you will.

1530
01:50:38,840 --> 01:50:47,080
But we can't enforce any of those people that live there to put any funds or anything onto this other lot.

1531
01:50:47,980 --> 01:50:51,440
I doubt any of them, any of them would do it and vice versa.

1532
01:50:51,860 --> 01:50:56,140
And thus, nor preventing them from using the paved road even if they don't want to put money onto it.

1533
01:50:56,760 --> 01:50:57,020
Right.

1534
01:50:57,140 --> 01:50:59,020
Like they can't put a gate up and say you need a code.

1535
01:50:59,280 --> 01:51:00,020
No, no, no, no, no.

1536
01:51:00,020 --> 01:51:00,240
Right.

1537
01:51:00,340 --> 01:51:03,000
No, because I can only imagine.

1538
01:51:03,840 --> 01:51:04,160
Yeah.

1539
01:51:04,320 --> 01:51:06,620
It would be a planning board stipulation that they can.

1540
01:51:06,620 --> 01:51:07,860
Well, I think it would have to be.

1541
01:51:07,960 --> 01:51:08,140
Right.

1542
01:51:08,140 --> 01:51:10,560
Because without it, they could in theory block that.

1543
01:51:10,600 --> 01:51:11,180
They could in theory block that.

1544
01:51:11,180 --> 01:51:11,360
Right.

1545
01:51:11,560 --> 01:51:13,260
And I think that would have to do that.

1546
01:51:13,280 --> 01:51:18,620
Just because there's a weird financial piece here that's like you're losing kind of part of a road, but they're gaining a much better road.

1547
01:51:18,880 --> 01:51:23,300
But like they're paying for this and then these people are going to move in and then they're going to want these people to pay for that section.

1548
01:51:23,560 --> 01:51:25,920
And it could just be a weird kind of argument.

1549
01:51:25,920 --> 01:51:27,700
I mean, I kind of look at it this way.

1550
01:51:29,640 --> 01:51:39,080
Someday, you know, 100 years ago, somebody who owned a farmhouse had plumbing installed in their house so they didn't have to use the outhouse anymore.

1551
01:51:39,080 --> 01:51:43,980
This new road is the new plumbing, right?

1552
01:51:44,620 --> 01:51:45,460
Yeah, that makes sense.

1553
01:51:45,920 --> 01:51:49,140
But what I was going to say is, yeah, I think it's going to be a private road.

1554
01:51:49,480 --> 01:51:49,740
Right.

1555
01:51:49,740 --> 01:52:00,940
And I have a feeling, again, just much like the other little mill, is that when this gets submitted as such, it'll need condominium documents that would have to get reviewed by town council, Diane Goro.

1556
01:52:02,220 --> 01:52:15,600
And my guess is that it will state that that that road section right there and all of Armand's way is up to the, let's just say because it's on the plan, seven units to pay for.

1557
01:52:15,720 --> 01:52:15,980
Yes.

1558
01:52:16,080 --> 01:52:19,000
Is it a bit of a burden on them that they're going to have?

1559
01:52:19,000 --> 01:52:19,460
That's what I think about.

1560
01:52:19,860 --> 01:52:20,100
Yeah.

1561
01:52:20,360 --> 01:52:20,640
Yeah.

1562
01:52:20,740 --> 01:52:22,280
But I've thought about that a lot.

1563
01:52:22,660 --> 01:52:22,940
You know?

1564
01:52:23,820 --> 01:52:38,020
I would encourage you, Ryan, to not do LCAs on this as a land condominium, but to use the open space development to do what we used to call, you know, a cluster.

1565
01:52:40,160 --> 01:52:42,000
Again, we don't even know what.

1566
01:52:43,040 --> 01:52:47,400
You just want to have a road, center line, center line, and that's why you're here.

1567
01:52:47,680 --> 01:52:48,300
That's correct.

1568
01:52:48,300 --> 01:53:00,580
Like I said, I don't know if these current owners are just going to sell it after I tell them, hey, the board is going like this, you know, or are they going to do it themselves because that's what they want to do.

1569
01:53:00,580 --> 01:53:03,320
I know they want resolution on the top piece.

1570
01:53:03,720 --> 01:53:04,800
It's not serving them.

1571
01:53:05,040 --> 01:53:06,720
It's not serving anyone.

1572
01:53:07,900 --> 01:53:09,380
It's all wooded.

1573
01:53:09,720 --> 01:53:15,900
And, you know, they drive through a small section of it on lot seven currently.

1574
01:53:16,300 --> 01:53:18,640
But other than that, it has no use.

1575
01:53:18,740 --> 01:53:20,760
Their cottages are all the way near Shoal Pond.

1576
01:53:20,860 --> 01:53:23,420
They're nowhere close to this open area.

1577
01:53:23,420 --> 01:53:30,280
So it's an orderly development, and what it looks like remains to be seen.

1578
01:53:31,000 --> 01:53:33,940
Well, Brian, would I – unless somebody – did you have something?

1579
01:53:34,100 --> 01:53:34,500
I don't know.

1580
01:53:34,660 --> 01:53:36,500
I'd rather see a public road, I think.

1581
01:53:37,060 --> 01:53:39,500
I don't see the advantage of doing a private road.

1582
01:53:39,500 --> 01:53:43,380
I think, like Steve said, like Stoneford was laid out.

1583
01:53:44,000 --> 01:53:44,520
Private.

1584
01:53:45,060 --> 01:53:48,040
It's – no, that's public over there, right?

1585
01:53:48,140 --> 01:53:49,340
It is after a point.

1586
01:53:49,720 --> 01:53:55,500
It's the lower point where it gets past the common, and then it becomes private.

1587
01:53:55,900 --> 01:53:56,580
Not Stoneford.

1588
01:53:56,740 --> 01:53:57,960
I mean, Waterford.

1589
01:53:58,300 --> 01:53:58,620
Waterford.

1590
01:53:58,900 --> 01:53:59,640
Gary's project.

1591
01:53:59,820 --> 01:54:00,660
Yeah, that's –

1592
01:54:00,660 --> 01:54:00,820
Yeah.

1593
01:54:01,200 --> 01:54:03,320
And it's reduced frontage, right?

1594
01:54:03,340 --> 01:54:04,640
125 feet or something?

1595
01:54:04,640 --> 01:54:21,200
Yeah, I mean, that was done under the old cluster ordinance, but, you know, Ryan is exploring the OSD ordinance, and, you know, the application he presented to you earlier this evening is –

1596
01:54:21,200 --> 01:54:30,180
I think he's got it figured out, and I'm not sure that the same recipe is the highest and best use on this piece, right?

1597
01:54:30,180 --> 01:54:34,200
I think it sounds like your remarks are on par with mine.

1598
01:54:35,600 --> 01:54:35,720
Yeah.

1599
01:54:35,920 --> 01:54:36,240
So that you create a –

1600
01:54:36,240 --> 01:54:40,260
And then there's no conflict with the – with a private road tying into a public road.

1601
01:54:40,260 --> 01:54:44,600
And I – I don't have a – would not have a problem with this being a public road.

1602
01:54:45,060 --> 01:54:45,460
Okay.

1603
01:54:45,580 --> 01:54:46,800
I think that would be an easier path.

1604
01:54:46,860 --> 01:54:49,020
Yeah, it eliminates a lot of the financial questions.

1605
01:54:49,020 --> 01:54:49,420
Yeah.

1606
01:54:49,420 --> 01:54:58,080
The only reason I usually say that is because it's – that becomes a sticking point sometimes for planning boards nowadays, but I understand the sentiment, and I took a note of that.

1607
01:54:58,080 --> 01:55:03,400
I like to think that in 2026 we've got our arms around how to –

1608
01:55:03,400 --> 01:55:04,060
Tackle it.

1609
01:55:04,060 --> 01:55:05,120
Public infrastructure here.

1610
01:55:05,300 --> 01:55:05,440
Yeah.

1611
01:55:05,640 --> 01:55:06,540
Yeah, no, I get it.

1612
01:55:06,660 --> 01:55:07,080
I get it.

1613
01:55:07,940 --> 01:55:15,920
And then so – sorry because it's not like Dubai, but the lower the existing homes there, that's all individual lots, or you're saying the owner of this –

1614
01:55:15,920 --> 01:55:20,240
So all of those – all those – do you see where they're hashed and they say existing dwelling?

1615
01:55:20,440 --> 01:55:20,580
Yeah.

1616
01:55:20,700 --> 01:55:25,720
Those are all – it's not shown on this, but they all are basically postage stamp lots.

1617
01:55:25,840 --> 01:55:30,700
Like there's a 100 by 100 box drawn around each – each one of these more or less.

1618
01:55:30,700 --> 01:55:31,760
Some of them have changed.

1619
01:55:32,080 --> 01:55:32,340
Okay.

1620
01:55:33,540 --> 01:55:46,920
Maybe I'll bring in, you know, the plan that Steve reviewed for the subdivision between the two of them, but it was an astronomical undertaking of surveying and trying to figure it out because there was like 12 lot line changes done between them in here.

1621
01:55:47,420 --> 01:55:51,100
It was – it was hectic, but –

1622
01:55:51,100 --> 01:55:55,740
But I mean, the people that own this land though, I mean, they own the land there?

1623
01:55:55,920 --> 01:55:57,400
Yeah, so they own –

1624
01:55:57,400 --> 01:55:57,900
Family store.

1625
01:55:57,900 --> 01:56:09,120
I don't have it right in front of me, but they own this 15 acres, and then there's, I don't know, I want to say maybe like 10 left over that just has four cottages on it, and it kind of meanders around every house.

1626
01:56:09,300 --> 01:56:16,220
When there's little tiny gaps where things don't touch around these houses, they own in between it, and it's pretty wacky.

1627
01:56:16,340 --> 01:56:17,860
Yeah, I was just trying to understand that.

1628
01:56:17,860 --> 01:56:19,200
The wackiest thing I've ever seen.

1629
01:56:19,260 --> 01:56:19,780
The story.

1630
01:56:20,220 --> 01:56:24,220
But there's a magical date, March 13th, 1956.

1631
01:56:24,220 --> 01:56:28,340
That's when Sandown adopted its first zoning ordinance.

1632
01:56:28,500 --> 01:56:30,540
All that was created before there was zoning.

1633
01:56:30,860 --> 01:56:31,140
Right.

1634
01:56:31,360 --> 01:56:31,580
Yeah.

1635
01:56:33,100 --> 01:56:53,540
Yeah, so we've tried to – like I alluded to in the beginning, I know it's been a while since I said it, but the intent of this family is eventually – I have not been contracted to do so, but to do a condominium site plan and come before the board for review of turning the four cottages that are just sitting on the big lot into a condominium site plan,

1636
01:56:53,540 --> 01:57:03,800
so that they can have individual ownership between sister, sister, brother, whatever, because right now it's still two parcels, same owner, just the family.

1637
01:57:03,800 --> 01:57:14,000
And when they have kids and grandkids, which they already have, things start to be an issue, as you can imagine, with who takes care of what, who pays taxes on this.

1638
01:57:14,320 --> 01:57:14,460
Yeah.

1639
01:57:14,500 --> 01:57:15,980
It's all a blur.

1640
01:57:16,420 --> 01:57:22,080
So they're trying to – they've been working hard for years on fixing this.

1641
01:57:22,760 --> 01:57:22,940
Okay.

1642
01:57:22,940 --> 01:57:25,040
And this is another step in fixing it, I think.

1643
01:57:26,120 --> 01:57:27,700
Ryan, I kind of agree with that.

1644
01:57:27,800 --> 01:57:30,020
It's a good improvement to the property.

1645
01:57:30,480 --> 01:57:30,660
Yeah.

1646
01:57:30,940 --> 01:57:34,760
Having a new road is good for everybody in there, whether it's private or public.

1647
01:57:34,760 --> 01:57:45,160
And if they've, you know, exhausted any legal land-use remedy in regards to the old part of the road that gets down to Little Mill.

1648
01:57:45,500 --> 01:57:45,720
Yeah.

1649
01:57:45,760 --> 01:57:48,520
But they're going to give everybody access to the new and improved one.

1650
01:57:48,900 --> 01:57:55,880
I don't have a big issue with the distance between the two of them if another plan like this moved forward.

1651
01:57:56,240 --> 01:57:56,540
Okay.

1652
01:57:56,620 --> 01:57:57,360
That's good to hear.

1653
01:57:57,440 --> 01:57:58,780
That's kind of why – that's why I'm here.

1654
01:57:59,020 --> 01:57:59,380
Yeah.

1655
01:57:59,380 --> 01:58:04,380
I think it's just – it's a better setup all around.

1656
01:58:04,960 --> 01:58:12,600
And I just have a feeling eventually everybody's going to forget about that access to Little Mill and it's – nature's going to sort of take it back.

1657
01:58:13,260 --> 01:58:16,360
The people down below are going to use it for their driveway like they always have.

1658
01:58:16,380 --> 01:58:21,560
Well, it's like they can stop maintaining that whole section of it, you know, if they wanted to.

1659
01:58:21,900 --> 01:58:28,420
And how they do that eventually is really for another meeting down the line if you're really just kind of concerned with that distance there.

1660
01:58:28,420 --> 01:58:30,180
That is what I'm concerned about.

1661
01:58:30,260 --> 01:58:37,440
It's the major sticking point because, like I said, I'll say it again, is that it costs a lot of money to develop land.

1662
01:58:38,460 --> 01:58:46,840
They're before the board a lot, but there's a lot of moving pieces and permit fees and this and the other thing and paying an engineer to do stormwater and, you know,

1663
01:58:46,840 --> 01:58:56,140
stormwater ponds and landscape plans and all this stuff and to go that mile and then give it to the board and have that waiver denied.

1664
01:58:56,140 --> 01:59:02,560
And you have no way to on the fly fix the plan to meet that regulation, which in this case, there is no way to fix it.

1665
01:59:02,560 --> 01:59:08,380
But it's just something that's been bothering the owners of this property for a long time.

1666
01:59:08,520 --> 01:59:10,960
And I kept going around and around.

1667
01:59:11,040 --> 01:59:15,300
I'm like, how am I supposed to tell you that this is going to work if I'm not submitting a package?

1668
01:59:15,300 --> 01:59:18,240
And how am I going to submit a package if we don't do all the work for it?

1669
01:59:18,620 --> 01:59:18,840
Yeah.

1670
01:59:19,100 --> 01:59:19,680
You know what I mean?

1671
01:59:19,680 --> 01:59:20,920
It's like a cat and a mouse game.

1672
01:59:21,240 --> 01:59:24,720
Yeah, that's what's great about a conceptual meeting, kind of feel out what's going on.

1673
01:59:24,920 --> 01:59:25,160
Yeah.

1674
01:59:25,280 --> 01:59:27,440
And I appreciate the listening to it.

1675
01:59:27,440 --> 01:59:29,840
And I know it's getting late for all of us.

1676
01:59:29,840 --> 01:59:30,240
It's all good.

1677
01:59:30,320 --> 01:59:31,460
No, it's interesting stuff.

1678
01:59:33,000 --> 01:59:37,640
And if we went with a public road on it, what's the concern there?

1679
01:59:37,780 --> 01:59:41,060
Like, you'd have less houses or what?

1680
01:59:41,060 --> 01:59:43,100
I don't think that there really would be a frontage or?

1681
01:59:43,600 --> 01:59:43,960
No.

1682
01:59:44,620 --> 01:59:44,820
No.

1683
01:59:45,220 --> 01:59:46,160
Or anything like that?

1684
01:59:48,520 --> 01:59:51,500
Usually it's up to the applicant what they're going to put in.

1685
01:59:51,560 --> 01:59:55,840
I think a lot of times where it looks like a cul-de-sac is usually a private road or a private way.

1686
01:59:56,840 --> 01:59:58,240
Would you just say a lot of planning?

1687
01:59:58,440 --> 01:59:59,680
You just typically.

1688
01:59:59,740 --> 01:59:59,920
Yeah.

1689
02:00:00,140 --> 02:00:09,520
A lot of the time, I guess in this case, sometimes when you're just doing, like, let's say a thousand foot road cul-de-sac,

1690
02:00:10,400 --> 02:00:16,640
some of the planning boards may say, how does that benefit the public if just the residents in there are going to come in there, turn around and go?

1691
02:00:16,900 --> 02:00:18,300
It's not a connecting road.

1692
02:00:18,840 --> 02:00:24,440
But in this case, it will be a public benefit because it's connecting there.

1693
02:00:24,440 --> 02:00:28,940
So I think it does have value as a town road, as Steve kind of said.

1694
02:00:28,940 --> 02:00:31,420
There's a cost of maintenance and stuff, too.

1695
02:00:31,420 --> 02:00:31,600
Right.

1696
02:00:31,760 --> 02:00:32,160
Yeah.

1697
02:00:32,360 --> 02:00:32,520
Yeah.

1698
02:00:32,800 --> 02:00:38,660
Because private, obviously, HOA has to come up with an agreement and they pool and they fix stuff.

1699
02:00:38,660 --> 02:00:45,120
But with the town, it's going to be, you know, take everything put in and then it gets conveyed to the town.

1700
02:00:45,120 --> 02:00:50,820
And I'm thinking that with new construction down in here, it's a half dozen knots or whatever it ends up going to be.

1701
02:00:50,960 --> 02:00:55,360
And this, what, maybe a thousand feet of road, probably less.

1702
02:00:55,860 --> 02:00:56,600
It could be less.

1703
02:00:56,660 --> 02:00:58,160
Like I said, we haven't gotten in the weeds.

1704
02:00:58,380 --> 02:01:03,520
I mean, those numbers from a performance standpoint on the amount of taxes those people are going to be paying.

1705
02:01:03,520 --> 02:01:05,740
It's a wash, kind of.

1706
02:01:06,180 --> 02:01:11,080
It's not going to be an issue.

1707
02:01:11,800 --> 02:01:12,020
Okay.

1708
02:01:12,080 --> 02:01:12,620
That's good to hear.

1709
02:01:12,620 --> 02:01:27,960
And to have private Shoal Pond Road terminate at a Class 5 road that is not a collector road is worth something to me.

1710
02:01:29,240 --> 02:01:29,440
Yeah.

1711
02:01:29,520 --> 02:01:30,100
No, I get it.

1712
02:01:30,260 --> 02:01:30,700
I get it.

1713
02:01:32,180 --> 02:01:37,040
I would encourage you to think about Armand's Way being a public road.

1714
02:01:37,460 --> 02:01:37,740
Yep.

1715
02:01:38,200 --> 02:01:40,300
I don't care if it's not, but as long as it is.

1716
02:01:40,300 --> 02:01:46,500
What has been said at the meeting tonight has made me think that it's a good idea, that it would benefit the town.

1717
02:01:46,660 --> 02:01:50,060
So I think that that's the approach that the owners should take.

1718
02:01:50,060 --> 02:01:53,780
Because we have something that we can maintain intersection to intersection.

1719
02:01:54,100 --> 02:01:54,400
Yes.

1720
02:01:54,920 --> 02:02:03,900
So that moving forward, that accelerates the redundancy of the existing intersection of.

1721
02:02:04,820 --> 02:02:05,060
Yeah.

1722
02:02:05,420 --> 02:02:08,620
And I'm sure all the owners in there will be happy about that.

1723
02:02:08,620 --> 02:02:11,320
I'm always going to use the road that's best maintained, right?

1724
02:02:11,760 --> 02:02:11,960
Yeah.

1725
02:02:12,440 --> 02:02:12,560
Yeah.

1726
02:02:13,880 --> 02:02:18,720
And when it's the town doing it rather than, you know, whoever does it down there.

1727
02:02:18,800 --> 02:02:19,560
I forget the guy's name.

1728
02:02:19,780 --> 02:02:20,180
Richie.

1729
02:02:20,440 --> 02:02:21,020
Yeah, Richie.

1730
02:02:21,140 --> 02:02:21,840
He's a good guy.

1731
02:02:21,940 --> 02:02:22,560
You need to meet him.

1732
02:02:22,960 --> 02:02:23,540
No, I met him.

1733
02:02:23,580 --> 02:02:24,120
He's a nice guy.

1734
02:02:26,500 --> 02:02:27,420
But, thank you.

1735
02:02:27,940 --> 02:02:30,460
Is there any other comments that I should take home with me?

1736
02:02:30,660 --> 02:02:31,360
Anybody else?

1737
02:02:31,360 --> 02:02:32,100
I think it's going to work.

1738
02:02:32,220 --> 02:02:33,700
I think what you have is great.

1739
02:02:35,020 --> 02:02:35,380
Yeah.

1740
02:02:35,960 --> 02:02:36,320
Okay.

1741
02:02:36,460 --> 02:02:38,420
If it is private though, then you've got to work out.

1742
02:02:39,560 --> 02:02:44,460
I don't, there's nothing that I'm seeing right now that's not leading me towards public

1743
02:02:44,460 --> 02:02:48,820
because of the talking myself into that it actually has public benefit to do a connection

1744
02:02:48,820 --> 02:02:50,180
of a street and stuff like that.

1745
02:02:50,180 --> 02:02:56,140
And I think that everyone in there would be thrilled by the introduction of, you know,

1746
02:02:56,700 --> 02:03:00,280
Sandown Highway Department when they've probably never seen those guys before.

1747
02:03:00,500 --> 02:03:00,780
Right.

1748
02:03:01,360 --> 02:03:01,800
You know.

1749
02:03:01,820 --> 02:03:02,200
Oh, yeah.

1750
02:03:02,440 --> 02:03:02,980
For sure.

1751
02:03:03,480 --> 02:03:05,520
Well, is that private still?

1752
02:03:05,640 --> 02:03:07,960
Is that the Gary Barnes development?

1753
02:03:08,040 --> 02:03:08,960
I know the one you're talking about.

1754
02:03:09,020 --> 02:03:10,140
Is that private or public now?

1755
02:03:10,340 --> 02:03:10,520
Is that?

1756
02:03:10,520 --> 02:03:11,100
No, that's public.

1757
02:03:11,280 --> 02:03:11,820
That's public.

1758
02:03:11,860 --> 02:03:12,340
That's a town.

1759
02:03:12,520 --> 02:03:13,640
It's the one on North Road?

1760
02:03:13,960 --> 02:03:14,240
Oh, yeah.

1761
02:03:14,260 --> 02:03:14,640
Yeah.

1762
02:03:15,180 --> 02:03:16,140
But did it start as private?

1763
02:03:16,140 --> 02:03:16,680
Got water fair?

1764
02:03:18,000 --> 02:03:19,060
Stoneford is private.

1765
02:03:19,480 --> 02:03:19,960
Stoneford.

1766
02:03:19,960 --> 02:03:20,780
It's still private.

1767
02:03:21,040 --> 02:03:22,420
We're talking about Waterford, right?

1768
02:03:22,420 --> 02:03:23,380
We're talking about Waterford.

1769
02:03:23,460 --> 02:03:26,040
Yeah, I misspoke when I said Stoneford in the beginning.

1770
02:03:26,260 --> 02:03:26,520
I'm watching.

1771
02:03:26,640 --> 02:03:27,660
But Waterford's public.

1772
02:03:28,000 --> 02:03:28,300
Yes.

1773
02:03:28,520 --> 02:03:28,740
Yes.

1774
02:03:28,740 --> 02:03:29,500
You can drive all over.

1775
02:03:29,580 --> 02:03:33,780
And then you'll see the overgrown recreation park that was put in there.

1776
02:03:33,980 --> 02:03:34,220
Right.

1777
02:03:34,440 --> 02:03:39,000
Which we didn't put it in because Gary Barnes said, what a shame.

1778
02:03:39,860 --> 02:03:40,080
Yeah.

1779
02:03:40,540 --> 02:03:41,340
We put it in.

1780
02:03:41,340 --> 02:03:42,900
It wasn't his fault.

1781
02:03:42,900 --> 02:03:43,660
It's overgrown.

1782
02:03:43,880 --> 02:03:45,260
It doesn't get maintained.

1783
02:03:45,520 --> 02:03:45,700
No.

1784
02:03:45,960 --> 02:03:46,500
Yeah, yeah.

1785
02:03:46,500 --> 02:03:47,380
But it's so clear.

1786
02:03:47,480 --> 02:03:48,060
That's at the end.

1787
02:03:48,160 --> 02:03:49,020
That's like, yeah.

1788
02:03:49,020 --> 02:03:50,560
I've been over there before, yeah.

1789
02:03:50,700 --> 02:03:53,140
People arguing about who's going to do this or that.

1790
02:03:53,460 --> 02:03:54,860
That was when Abdullah bought it.

1791
02:03:55,040 --> 02:03:56,300
That's why we went to...

1792
02:03:56,300 --> 02:03:57,560
During the downturn, we designed that.

1793
02:03:58,260 --> 02:03:59,200
Well, it was...

1794
02:03:59,200 --> 02:04:00,440
Gary had a really nice setup.

1795
02:04:00,900 --> 02:04:01,340
Sublime.

1796
02:04:01,540 --> 02:04:01,700
Yeah.

1797
02:04:01,840 --> 02:04:02,040
Keith.

1798
02:04:02,100 --> 02:04:03,100
Sublime did that.

1799
02:04:03,100 --> 02:04:05,800
And did we do the survey?

1800
02:04:06,120 --> 02:04:06,860
Yeah, that's right.

1801
02:04:06,940 --> 02:04:08,120
When we had the recession.

1802
02:04:08,200 --> 02:04:08,520
I think you did.

1803
02:04:08,580 --> 02:04:09,160
I think we did.

1804
02:04:09,220 --> 02:04:10,680
We always did Gary Barnes stuff.

1805
02:04:11,640 --> 02:04:13,980
No, but the guy who works for me was the design engineer.

1806
02:04:14,200 --> 02:04:14,440
Yeah.

1807
02:04:14,820 --> 02:04:16,120
You told me he works for you now.

1808
02:04:16,660 --> 02:04:17,920
20 plus years ago.

1809
02:04:18,560 --> 02:04:19,160
Nice homes.

1810
02:04:19,660 --> 02:04:20,880
People are very happy with them.

1811
02:04:22,220 --> 02:04:22,400
Yeah.

1812
02:04:22,580 --> 02:04:22,960
Okay.

1813
02:04:23,640 --> 02:04:23,920
All right.

1814
02:04:23,980 --> 02:04:24,340
Thank you.

1815
02:04:24,500 --> 02:04:25,340
Members of the board of that.

1816
02:04:25,560 --> 02:04:26,040
Thank you.

1817
02:04:26,640 --> 02:04:27,160
Thank you.

1818
02:04:27,180 --> 02:04:27,740
Enjoy your trip.

1819
02:04:27,940 --> 02:04:28,100
Thanks.

1820
02:04:28,200 --> 02:04:28,380
Yep.

1821
02:04:28,440 --> 02:04:28,840
Thank you.

1822
02:04:28,900 --> 02:04:30,460
And thanks for listening to me all night.

1823
02:04:30,700 --> 02:04:31,700
Don't send us a postcard.

1824
02:04:31,760 --> 02:04:32,340
When are you leaving?

1825
02:04:32,980 --> 02:04:33,420
Thursday.

1826
02:04:34,420 --> 02:04:35,860
Like, the day after tomorrow?

1827
02:04:36,000 --> 02:04:36,200
Yeah.

1828
02:04:36,820 --> 02:04:37,400
And then I come back.

1829
02:04:37,400 --> 02:04:38,620
You get to go to Italy on Thursday.

1830
02:04:38,740 --> 02:04:39,900
I get to go to Thornton.

1831
02:04:39,960 --> 02:04:40,140
Yeah.

1832
02:04:40,200 --> 02:04:41,080
I get to go to Rome.

1833
02:04:41,340 --> 02:04:42,140
It's going right.

1834
02:04:42,500 --> 02:04:42,940
Serenno.

1835
02:04:43,420 --> 02:04:43,600
Yeah.

1836
02:04:44,320 --> 02:04:44,680
Nice.

1837
02:04:45,020 --> 02:04:45,800
See Pompeii.

1838
02:04:46,020 --> 02:04:46,440
It'll be cool.

1839
02:04:46,740 --> 02:04:47,120
Be safe.

1840
02:04:48,340 --> 02:04:48,880
Thank you.

1841
02:04:49,000 --> 02:04:49,500
Appreciate it.

1842
02:04:49,760 --> 02:04:50,140
Thank you.

1843
02:04:50,140 --> 02:04:50,560
Enjoy.

1844
02:04:51,700 --> 02:04:52,380
All right.

1845
02:04:53,320 --> 02:04:53,540
Yeah.

1846
02:04:53,680 --> 02:04:54,280
That was great.

1847
02:04:54,460 --> 02:04:54,820
Thank you.

1848
02:04:54,820 --> 02:05:00,220
I was going to spend a couple of minutes with some running through new or old business.

1849
02:05:00,420 --> 02:05:04,660
So I think we brought up one or two points that we need to take a few minutes to discuss

1850
02:05:04,660 --> 02:05:05,060
tonight.

1851
02:05:06,520 --> 02:05:08,540
But they may take a few minutes to do.

1852
02:05:08,740 --> 02:05:10,900
I had a couple that are more like bullet points.

1853
02:05:11,000 --> 02:05:15,100
I just want to bring the board up to speed on and then we'll maybe have a discussion about

1854
02:05:15,100 --> 02:05:20,540
direct fees and different voting procedures.

1855
02:05:20,540 --> 02:05:26,000
First thing I just wanted to let everybody know, we have to get into the budget review

1856
02:05:26,000 --> 02:05:27,340
that's coming up.

1857
02:05:27,660 --> 02:05:32,940
I have to, well, each board has to do a budget review and submit it to the selectmen.

1858
02:05:33,060 --> 02:05:36,940
So I think planning has to have ours in by October 26th.

1859
02:05:36,940 --> 02:05:39,860
I didn't see anything in anybody's packages.

1860
02:05:40,120 --> 02:05:44,420
Usually they'll give us a copy of last year's or the last few years so that we can kind of

1861
02:05:44,420 --> 02:05:45,800
take a look, make a few notes.

1862
02:05:45,800 --> 02:05:51,180
So what I'll do is I'll ask Andrea to see if she can dig one up and email it to everybody.

1863
02:05:52,260 --> 02:05:57,640
We can take a look at it and then we'll have to deal with it at the next meeting on the 20th.

1864
02:05:58,660 --> 02:06:00,500
It's usually, it's relatively simple.

1865
02:06:00,860 --> 02:06:02,280
We have a very low budget.

1866
02:06:02,280 --> 02:06:09,120
But even if we eliminate it, it doesn't move the town's finances at all, you know?

1867
02:06:09,360 --> 02:06:12,680
So we just try to adjust where we can with it.

1868
02:06:12,680 --> 02:06:22,780
If we were going to propose any zoning amendments for the coming year, we'd have to start doing

1869
02:06:22,780 --> 02:06:24,240
some workshops on them now.

1870
02:06:25,080 --> 02:06:26,520
So I wanted to put that out.

1871
02:06:26,600 --> 02:06:32,340
If anybody has any ideas about cleaning up any existing regs or proposing new zoning amendments,

1872
02:06:33,080 --> 02:06:35,460
we would have to get rolling with it shortly.

1873
02:06:35,760 --> 02:06:41,080
I got a publication today from Diane's office.

1874
02:06:41,080 --> 02:06:43,300
I think you probably have in your inbox, too.

1875
02:06:43,680 --> 02:06:44,360
Maybe, yeah.

1876
02:06:44,520 --> 02:06:50,480
That's a summary of outcome of the current legislative session.

1877
02:06:51,060 --> 02:06:51,360
Okay.

1878
02:06:51,640 --> 02:07:00,820
And with sort of some suggestions that what municipalities may want to look to.

1879
02:07:02,100 --> 02:07:08,260
I'm certain that Andrea has that.

1880
02:07:08,260 --> 02:07:14,420
If, just to belt and suspenders, John, I'll forward it to you.

1881
02:07:14,780 --> 02:07:15,180
Okay.

1882
02:07:15,620 --> 02:07:17,460
And you can forward it on to the others.

1883
02:07:17,760 --> 02:07:17,960
Yeah.

1884
02:07:18,120 --> 02:07:21,120
It was, I haven't read it all, but it was well done as it always is.

1885
02:07:21,200 --> 02:07:22,720
You know, she does it every year.

1886
02:07:22,900 --> 02:07:23,140
Yeah.

1887
02:07:23,380 --> 02:07:23,580
Yeah.

1888
02:07:23,580 --> 02:07:28,640
You know, the only other thing that we had held over from last year was the commercial

1889
02:07:28,640 --> 02:07:32,600
or the business district and some conversations about different uses there.

1890
02:07:33,420 --> 02:07:37,480
But that project that was spawned that is still out there.

1891
02:07:37,540 --> 02:07:42,960
So I don't know if we want to leave here and touch that at this point until that's disposed of.

1892
02:07:42,960 --> 02:07:46,300
So, yeah, I don't want to make any changes to anything that's ongoing.

1893
02:07:46,600 --> 02:07:55,200
But, you know, I always have that little hit list of things that I keep in tab notes and copying my side of the ordinance.

1894
02:07:55,300 --> 02:07:56,560
I don't remember right now.

1895
02:07:57,160 --> 02:07:57,440
Okay.

1896
02:07:57,540 --> 02:07:58,240
I'm kind of tired.

1897
02:07:58,240 --> 02:08:10,080
But I would want, as far as potential zoning amendments, to look at the outcome of the legislative session,

1898
02:08:10,180 --> 02:08:13,600
much like you did last year with the ADUs.

1899
02:08:14,020 --> 02:08:14,240
Yeah.

1900
02:08:15,000 --> 02:08:20,860
I mean, change in statute compelled change in the zoning ordinance to accommodate ADUs.

1901
02:08:21,980 --> 02:08:22,160
Yeah.

1902
02:08:22,160 --> 02:08:30,140
So, with that, John, I'm going to, unless you've got something for me, I'm going to take off.

1903
02:08:30,260 --> 02:08:33,420
But I will get you that email from Diane.

1904
02:08:33,800 --> 02:08:34,040
Yep.

1905
02:08:34,120 --> 02:08:34,780
That would be great.

1906
02:08:34,860 --> 02:08:35,960
I'll circulate it around.

1907
02:08:36,200 --> 02:08:38,400
But I just wanted to put that on everybody's radar.

1908
02:08:39,600 --> 02:08:44,760
Zoning amendment time is coming up, and we can start scheduling some workshops if anybody has something to work on.

1909
02:08:45,420 --> 02:08:47,160
Do we have, like, a time frame on that?

1910
02:08:47,260 --> 02:08:48,220
Like, a rough idea?

1911
02:08:48,700 --> 02:08:50,840
It's for March is when the town vote.

1912
02:08:50,840 --> 02:08:51,820
So, we have to have.

1913
02:08:52,320 --> 02:08:53,280
We have to post.

1914
02:08:53,400 --> 02:08:54,780
January 20th, is it?

1915
02:08:54,800 --> 02:09:00,020
We have to post for public hearing in December.

1916
02:09:02,120 --> 02:09:07,760
The calendar, the statutory calendar, is available.

1917
02:09:08,020 --> 02:09:11,000
It's on the New Hampshire Municipal Association website.

1918
02:09:11,460 --> 02:09:14,980
I think it's also on the Rockingham Planning Commission website.

1919
02:09:14,980 --> 02:09:19,940
I haven't looked at it because I haven't looked at it.

1920
02:09:20,000 --> 02:09:20,980
I've got enough shit to do.

1921
02:09:21,100 --> 02:09:25,660
Yeah, just curious as to, like, how much time we've got to have some scheduled workshop meeting.

1922
02:09:25,660 --> 02:09:38,780
What you really want to do, in my opinion, is like, you know, in October, have a work session where you're here.

1923
02:09:38,780 --> 02:09:45,340
John's done these many times where you're here, and you have conversation of what-if scenarios, right?

1924
02:09:45,660 --> 02:09:47,280
What do you want to work on, if anything?

1925
02:09:48,620 --> 02:09:50,920
You might have some things that are compulsory.

1926
02:09:51,880 --> 02:09:53,800
Everything else, not so much, right?

1927
02:09:53,800 --> 02:09:58,200
Yeah, Diane's letter would show us if there's something we really have to get rolling with.

1928
02:09:58,700 --> 02:10:04,600
And then if you decide that you want to do something that, you know, like we did with the ADUs last year,

1929
02:10:04,700 --> 02:10:09,640
between Rockingham and myself, we put something together and get it in front of you,

1930
02:10:09,680 --> 02:10:11,340
and then you can have at it, right?

1931
02:10:12,020 --> 02:10:16,940
It's not as complicated as you might think if you understand the law.

1932
02:10:17,180 --> 02:10:17,380
Sure.

1933
02:10:17,380 --> 02:10:17,560
Right.

1934
02:10:18,580 --> 02:10:20,600
And then some things do get complicated.

1935
02:10:21,920 --> 02:10:22,280
Yeah.

1936
02:10:22,280 --> 02:10:24,000
Okay, go ahead.

1937
02:10:24,100 --> 02:10:27,240
Being 63 years old and having this four nights this week.

1938
02:10:27,360 --> 02:10:27,740
Yeah, yeah.

1939
02:10:28,060 --> 02:10:29,580
I'm halfway there, guys.

1940
02:10:30,000 --> 02:10:30,420
All right.

1941
02:10:30,720 --> 02:10:31,360
Thanks, Steve.

1942
02:10:31,560 --> 02:10:32,060
Thank you.

1943
02:10:32,060 --> 02:10:32,140
Thank you.

1944
02:10:32,140 --> 02:10:32,940
I appreciate it.

1945
02:10:33,040 --> 02:10:33,660
Have a good night.

1946
02:10:36,160 --> 02:10:36,440
Let's see.

1947
02:10:37,060 --> 02:10:39,420
Next thing, I just wanted to put on everybody's radar.

1948
02:10:39,720 --> 02:10:42,080
I got an email from Lynn the other day.

1949
02:10:43,040 --> 02:10:45,800
The town had to update a hazard mitigation plan.

1950
02:10:46,280 --> 02:10:47,980
It was kind of a last-minute thing.

1951
02:10:47,980 --> 02:10:51,540
It must be time-sensitive, and I have a feeling somebody forgot about it.

1952
02:10:51,540 --> 02:11:01,180
So she asked me to sit in on a meeting this Thursday around 1.30 with the RPC, her, police, and fire.

1953
02:11:02,460 --> 02:11:02,940
TBW.

1954
02:11:03,200 --> 02:11:03,680
TBW.

1955
02:11:04,080 --> 02:11:04,260
Yeah.

1956
02:11:04,760 --> 02:11:04,960
Yeah.

1957
02:11:05,060 --> 02:11:06,560
I'm sure there's going to be a selectman there.

1958
02:11:06,760 --> 02:11:06,940
Yeah.

1959
02:11:07,260 --> 02:11:07,580
All right.

1960
02:11:07,740 --> 02:11:08,380
Two of them, maybe.

1961
02:11:08,380 --> 02:11:12,600
So I'll update everybody next month on what that meeting is.

1962
02:11:12,680 --> 02:11:15,720
I got emailed the previous plan.

1963
02:11:17,280 --> 02:11:19,380
It's going to take me a little time to run through it.

1964
02:11:19,380 --> 02:11:21,260
I haven't had a chance to look at it either.

1965
02:11:21,660 --> 02:11:22,400
Excuse me.

1966
02:11:23,200 --> 02:11:27,340
But I imagine it's just going to be some kind of a review and update if there's anything significant.

1967
02:11:28,620 --> 02:11:32,060
Either way, I'll let everybody know what goes on with that next month.

1968
02:11:32,060 --> 02:11:38,000
And that's really it.

1969
02:11:38,140 --> 02:11:50,140
I was at an RPC meeting this past week, and the only big thing that really came from that, it was some votes on some state roads, some different funding for them.

1970
02:11:50,140 --> 02:11:56,600
We really only have one state road, so there really aren't a lot of projects for Sandown on it.

1971
02:11:57,480 --> 02:12:09,780
But one sort of big thing that was going on is they have various plans, like a 10-year plan, a 5-year plan, like a 30-year plan for different road improvements, bridge, infrastructure stuff.

1972
02:12:10,880 --> 02:12:17,480
And I guess a lot of the money and the funding for their 10-year plan got vetoed by the governor.

1973
02:12:17,480 --> 02:12:30,740
And then, I guess, the state reps must have voted to override the veto so that that money came back into the RPC so that it can kind of go out there and do road improvements.

1974
02:12:31,700 --> 02:12:41,660
But what they did say is any town that does have a few things on the agenda, and I know I had submitted stuff a while back, they have to keep following up with it.

1975
02:12:42,300 --> 02:12:46,000
And they're discouraging people from trying to add on for budgetary reasons.

1976
02:12:46,000 --> 02:12:48,580
There's just not a lot of money or funding out there right now.

1977
02:12:48,900 --> 02:12:56,240
If there's any way to consolidate stuff that you have, maybe, like, group it together to make it a little more economically feasible.

1978
02:12:56,240 --> 02:12:58,500
They were coming up with suggestions like that.

1979
02:12:59,300 --> 02:13:09,460
But they also said if any town's not really checking in and seeing where their project is, it might just disappear, and they'll just consider it abandoned, you know?

1980
02:13:09,460 --> 02:13:25,580
So I'm going to make a point of just double-checking and see what has been on the radar for Sandown, what was pending, if anything, and then just make sure that we stay up on it so it doesn't vanish on us.

1981
02:13:25,580 --> 02:13:30,460
Do we have some bridge work that's planned?

1982
02:13:30,900 --> 02:13:32,280
That was right over there, right?

1983
02:13:33,900 --> 02:13:34,260
What's that?

1984
02:13:34,440 --> 02:13:35,960
Was it down Main Street or something?

1985
02:13:35,960 --> 02:13:36,980
No, that bridge was done.

1986
02:13:37,300 --> 02:13:37,760
That was.

1987
02:13:38,360 --> 02:13:40,060
That was redone, what, 20 years ago?

1988
02:13:40,540 --> 02:13:42,880
The one on, I'm not sure.

1989
02:13:42,880 --> 02:13:49,920
But I thought we had some stuff that had to be, that the state was going to put money into.

1990
02:13:50,180 --> 02:13:53,380
No, right, we're funding that, but that's not state highway.

1991
02:13:55,380 --> 02:13:56,540
Fremont Road was done.

1992
02:13:57,520 --> 02:13:58,800
We've got one more to do.

1993
02:14:00,200 --> 02:14:05,560
But anyways, I thought there was a couple projects in town with Bridges that needed that.

1994
02:14:05,820 --> 02:14:10,320
That state, the state, we were supposed to put part of the money in, be putting part of the money away for.

1995
02:14:10,740 --> 02:14:11,460
We had to have.

1996
02:14:11,460 --> 02:14:13,340
And then they were going to chip in for.

1997
02:14:13,340 --> 02:14:15,400
We had to have 100% of the money before they.

1998
02:14:15,500 --> 02:14:16,280
Oh, is that what it was?

1999
02:14:16,720 --> 02:14:22,080
Appropriate their 100%, well, 50%, whatever the numbers were.

2000
02:14:23,880 --> 02:14:25,860
You have to have, the town has to have.

2001
02:14:25,900 --> 02:14:27,580
Right, that's why we vote on it every year.

2002
02:14:27,640 --> 02:14:33,380
We'll put in $30,000, $50,000 towards a million dollars to replace whatever we were replacing.

2003
02:14:33,740 --> 02:14:37,840
We had to have X dollars before the state would kick in their money.

2004
02:14:38,220 --> 02:14:38,560
Gotcha.

2005
02:14:38,660 --> 02:14:41,420
Because if we didn't raise the money, the money goes away.

2006
02:14:42,340 --> 02:14:45,780
You could be, the state doesn't know from year to year if they're going to have money.

2007
02:14:46,160 --> 02:14:46,420
Right.

2008
02:14:46,840 --> 02:14:47,020
Right.

2009
02:14:47,020 --> 02:14:48,440
So, well, basically what I was.

2010
02:14:48,440 --> 02:14:49,400
So, there might have been a couple things.

2011
02:14:49,540 --> 02:14:49,860
Yeah.

2012
02:14:50,040 --> 02:14:51,440
So, I'm going to try to look on those.

2013
02:14:51,520 --> 02:15:00,220
And then I'm going to talk, you know, again with Steve and the road agent to see if they have any other projects that they wanted to try to work with.

2014
02:15:01,040 --> 02:15:03,340
Even if we just sort of get it on the radar a little.

2015
02:15:03,620 --> 02:15:05,720
Did they say they buy equipment at all?

2016
02:15:06,820 --> 02:15:08,200
They weren't talking about equipment.

2017
02:15:08,420 --> 02:15:11,320
They were just, it was really just more budgetary stuff.

2018
02:15:11,560 --> 02:15:14,760
They really didn't get into a list of projects or anything.

2019
02:15:14,840 --> 02:15:15,380
I'm just wondering.

2020
02:15:15,380 --> 02:15:15,820
Yeah.

2021
02:15:17,820 --> 02:15:19,700
So, that's the stuff that I had.

2022
02:15:20,460 --> 02:15:27,260
And if you guys want to kind of talk about the donations for rec and the kind of the history or background of that.

2023
02:15:27,260 --> 02:15:30,260
I'm definitely open to it.

2024
02:15:30,680 --> 02:15:47,240
What I would ask, though, with any of the new members, if there is any questions about that, concerns, any of the processes that we use, or, you know, just some of these, you know, items like the recreation fee.

2025
02:15:47,940 --> 02:15:51,000
If we can talk about it maybe before the meeting, have a call.

2026
02:15:51,140 --> 02:15:53,440
But I don't want to have discussions like that.

2027
02:15:53,500 --> 02:15:55,200
We can set up a workshop if we need to.

2028
02:15:55,200 --> 02:16:04,060
But I don't want to stop each public hearing and kind of get into a larger debate about some of the administrative stuff that we do.

2029
02:16:04,500 --> 02:16:09,160
So, even a phone call to different board members, if something's on the radar, say, hey, what is this?

2030
02:16:09,200 --> 02:16:10,100
Or how does that work?

2031
02:16:10,920 --> 02:16:16,900
That might be a little easier for us to get through the public hearings because they do take up enough time.

2032
02:16:17,800 --> 02:16:21,580
And I'd like to try to just keep it as efficient as I can as the chair.

2033
02:16:21,580 --> 02:16:31,380
And, you know, just keep things moving and really focus the time on more of the land use pieces of it that we need to get through and vote on, you know?

2034
02:16:32,340 --> 02:16:33,300
I'm all for efficiency.

2035
02:16:33,440 --> 02:16:34,420
So, I apologize for bringing it up.

2036
02:16:34,460 --> 02:16:38,020
I just want to get too far ahead without having to talk about it.

2037
02:16:38,340 --> 02:16:38,560
Right.

2038
02:16:38,560 --> 02:16:43,840
So, if you want to talk about it, I have no problem with the waiver.

2039
02:16:44,920 --> 02:16:49,960
Well, like, the waiver, I completely understand why people would require it or request it.

2040
02:16:51,220 --> 02:16:58,360
My real concern is optically what the voluntary contribution looks like.

2041
02:16:58,360 --> 02:17:03,020
So, like, to me in my head, if it's voluntary, why is it always $1,000?

2042
02:17:04,000 --> 02:17:13,820
So, like, the 21 lots, $1,000 per lot, $21,000 to the revolving fund, that's like a giant business that's giving $1,000 per lot.

2043
02:17:13,900 --> 02:17:14,720
$1,000 is a lot of money.

2044
02:17:15,500 --> 02:17:17,060
Hershey Road did the same thing.

2045
02:17:17,160 --> 02:17:19,560
Two lots, it's a private individual, it's $1,000.

2046
02:17:20,380 --> 02:17:23,840
Why was he not like, I don't have $100, $10 I'll contribute.

2047
02:17:24,320 --> 02:17:25,760
Why is it always $1,000?

2048
02:17:25,760 --> 02:17:34,640
So, like, maybe it started out as, like, a great, like, voluntary, I'm going to voluntarily, you know, give $1,000 a long time ago.

2049
02:17:34,800 --> 02:17:40,380
But it seems to be more of a, like, you know, I have to give $1,000.

2050
02:17:40,580 --> 02:17:46,420
I don't know if we're not, maybe we're not making it clear enough to individuals that it can be any amount or no amount.

2051
02:17:46,980 --> 02:17:51,760
You know, maybe there has to be some sort of language put in there that kind of absolves us.

2052
02:17:51,760 --> 02:18:00,920
Or it just seems like $1,000 has become routine, and routine can probably lead to a slippery slope of, I give them $1,000, I get a waiver.

2053
02:18:01,920 --> 02:18:02,080
Right.

2054
02:18:02,080 --> 02:18:11,860
The past few years, on single lots, two lots, and even three lots, we were waiving it.

2055
02:18:12,180 --> 02:18:12,460
Yeah.

2056
02:18:13,720 --> 02:18:16,700
Not in all cases, some just wanted to give us the money, which is fine.

2057
02:18:16,800 --> 02:18:18,580
Which is fine, yeah, for sure.

2058
02:18:18,580 --> 02:18:24,680
But in most cases, I started this 20 years ago, maybe.

2059
02:18:25,200 --> 02:18:35,500
So, was it a, was it a, what's it, did the town say if you contribute voluntarily $1,000, it would help us?

2060
02:18:35,620 --> 02:18:37,000
Or, because it's that conversation.

2061
02:18:37,220 --> 02:18:42,920
So, if an applicant comes up with it, I'm going to voluntarily do this regardless of how you give me the waiver.

2062
02:18:43,060 --> 02:18:43,640
That's different.

2063
02:18:43,640 --> 02:18:53,260
But if we, if the town a long time ago was involved in the conversation that says, hey, if you contribute voluntarily a certain amount of money, or, you know,

2064
02:18:53,660 --> 02:18:57,060
there's a weird conversation that could have happened 20 years ago that maybe none of us were a part of,

2065
02:18:57,400 --> 02:19:01,200
but it got to a point now where it might seem too routine to be voluntary.

2066
02:19:02,040 --> 02:19:03,960
I wasn't here when it started.

2067
02:19:04,100 --> 02:19:06,080
I know I didn't speak more to it.

2068
02:19:06,080 --> 02:19:15,440
But I think what happened was, there's a state regulation that says, if you're going to build a subdivision, you have to have a recreational area in it.

2069
02:19:15,900 --> 02:19:19,740
But I think what they found, unfortunately, is that no one maintains it.

2070
02:19:19,880 --> 02:19:24,260
It turns into a financial draw for that subdivision.

2071
02:19:24,260 --> 02:19:27,560
It turns into an eyesore a lot of times.

2072
02:19:27,720 --> 02:19:29,600
It's expensive to put those in.

2073
02:19:30,540 --> 02:19:36,880
So, I think both the town, and it seems like the town and the builders were looking for another alternative.

2074
02:19:37,480 --> 02:19:41,220
But I think it was legally difficult to try to do something.

2075
02:19:42,020 --> 02:19:52,060
So, I think the donation is what passed legal muster at the time and seemed to work out good for both the town and the builders.

2076
02:19:52,060 --> 02:20:00,220
You know, the only thing that I had with the $1,000, in my mind, has always been, if that got started 25 years ago,

2077
02:20:00,700 --> 02:20:06,040
I think this is kind of what Lisa started to say, there should have been some gradual increase.

2078
02:20:06,280 --> 02:20:09,280
But you can't also, because it's a donation, you can't do that.

2079
02:20:09,620 --> 02:20:17,380
So, I don't know if there's a legal way to put something into, like, the town regulations about that.

2080
02:20:17,380 --> 02:20:18,140
Maybe Diane could weigh in.

2081
02:20:18,140 --> 02:20:22,180
But I think the reason they never did is because there isn't a way to do it.

2082
02:20:22,280 --> 02:20:26,240
And that was the donation thing is what kind of tied it all together.

